<div class="editorial-layout"><div class="chapter-list"><details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-1" open><summary><span class="chapter-count">01</span><h3>The plan for your space</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><p>Littleton covers more ground, literally, than a lot of homeowners realize when they start comparing xeriscape contractors. A century-old lot near Historic Downtown Littleton has different soil, tree cover, and drainage than a newer property out toward Ken Caryl, closer to the foothills. Picking a xeriscape contractor who treats those as the same project is how conversions end up underperforming.</p>

<p>Ridgewell Landscape and Design has spent over a decade working across the Denver metro, and Littleton’s range of older, established neighborhoods and foothill-adjacent developments is something we’ve designed around many times.</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-2"><summary><span class="chapter-count">02</span><h3>What Should You Look for in a Xeriscape Contractor?</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><p>A good xeriscape contractor does more than install plants. They assess your soil and drainage first, design irrigation specifically for the plants they’re recommending rather than reusing your old sprinkler layout, and stick around for maintenance while the landscape establishes. In Littleton specifically, that also means understanding whether you’re working with rockier, foothill-adjacent soil or older, more established garden beds that need careful integration rather than a full teardown.</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-3"><summary><span class="chapter-count">03</span><h3>Why Littleton Homeowners Call In Dedicated Xeriscape Contractors</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><p>The City of Littleton runs its own Water Conservation Program with Resource Central, offering residents lawn replacement support and discounted native plant kits. That kind of consistent municipal backing reflects a real, ongoing water concern rather than a short-term push.</p>

<p>Littleton’s older neighborhoods add another layer. A lot of established properties already have mature trees and shrubs worth preserving, which means a good xeriscape contractor needs to integrate new, low-water plantings around existing landscaping, not just clear-cut the whole yard and start over.</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-4"><summary><span class="chapter-count">04</span><h3>Our Xeriscape Contractor Services in Littleton</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><h3><strong>Working Around Established Landscaping</strong></h3>

<p>For older properties, we design conversions that preserve mature trees and worthwhile existing plants while replacing the water-hungry lawn around them.</p>

<h3><strong>Foothill-Adjacent Design</strong></h3>

<p>For properties closer to Ken Caryl and the foothills, we account for rockier soil and different drainage patterns than you’d find in Littleton’s flatter, older neighborhoods.</p>

<h3><strong>Full Installation</strong></h3>

<p>Grading, irrigation, hardscaping, and planting are all handled directly by our team, typically led by Francisco Garcia and lead designer Chris, rather than handed off to separate design and install crews.</p>

<h3><strong>Ongoing Maintenance</strong></h3>

<p>New xeriscapes need attention during their first year or two while roots establish. We provide maintenance to protect that investment long after installation.</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-5"><summary><span class="chapter-count">05</span><h3>Signs You Need a Xeriscape Contractor in Littleton</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><ul>
<li>Your established landscaping has mature trees you want to keep, but the lawn around them isn’t working</li>
<li>You’re near the foothills and dealing with rocky, uneven soil that standard landscaping crews struggle with</li>
<li>Your water bill spikes every summer despite a modest-sized yard</li>
<li>Your lawn has thinned out over the years and simple reseeding hasn’t fixed it</li>
<li>You want a contractor who handles design, installation, and maintenance rather than just one piece of the job</li>
</ul>
</div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-6"><summary><span class="chapter-count">06</span><h3>Water Conservation Support Available in Littleton</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><p>Littleton residents have access to water conservation support through the city’s partnership with Resource Central, which can help offset part of a lawn replacement project. Program terms change over time, so we recommend confirming current details, and we can point you toward current options during your estimate.</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-7"><summary><span class="chapter-count">07</span><h3>Why Littleton Homeowners Choose Ridgewell as Their Xeriscape Contractor</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><p>Ridgewell Landscape and Design was founded in 2011 by longtime Colorado resident Francisco Garcia, and more than a decade of experience across this region’s varied terrain, from established city lots to foothill-adjacent properties, shapes how we approach every Littleton project. We’re licensed and insured, hold a perfect 5.0-star rating on Google, and keep our team small enough that Francisco and Chris are personally involved in most of the work.</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-8"><summary><span class="chapter-count">08</span><h3>Get Your Free Estimate from a Littleton Xeriscape Contractor</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><p>Whether your property is a century-old lot downtown or a newer build near the foothills, let’s talk about what will actually work there.<a href="/contact/"> Get Your Free Estimate</a> and our team will follow up.</p>

<hr/></div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-9"><summary><span class="chapter-count">09</span><h3>Your questions, answered</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><h3><strong>What should I look for when hiring a xeriscape contractor in Littleton?</strong></h3>

<p>Look for a contractor who assesses your soil and drainage before recommending plants, designs irrigation specifically for the new landscape rather than reusing old sprinkler heads, and offers maintenance after installation. A contractor who treats every yard the same regardless of soil or terrain is a warning sign.</p>

<h3><strong>Does xeriscaping work well near Littleton’s foothills, like Ken Caryl?</strong> </h3>

<p>Yes, though the design approach shifts. Foothill-adjacent soil tends to be rockier with different drainage patterns than Littleton’s flatter, older neighborhoods, so plant selection and grading need to reflect that rather than following a one-size-fits-all plan.</p>

<h3><strong>Can a xeriscape conversion preserve my mature trees and shrubs?</strong> </h3>

<p>In most cases, yes. A well-planned conversion works around established trees and worthwhile existing plants rather than clearing everything, integrating new low-water plantings into what’s already there.</p>

<h3><strong>What is Littleton’s Water Conservation Program?</strong> </h3>

<p>It’s a city partnership with Resource Central that offers residents support for lawn replacement projects, including discounted native plant kits. Program details change periodically, so it’s worth confirming current terms directly.</p>

<h3><strong>Is xeriscaping different from simply reducing the size of my lawn?</strong> </h3>

<p>Yes. Simply shrinking a lawn without changing the plants or irrigation still leaves you with a water-intensive area, just a smaller one. A proper xeriscape conversion replaces the plant palette and irrigation approach entirely in the converted area.</p>

<h3><strong>Can I hire a xeriscape contractor to do my project in phases?</strong> </h3>

<p>Yes, and many Littleton homeowners prefer this approach, starting with the front yard or a single problem area and expanding to the rest of the property over time.</p>
</div>
</details>
